<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sadriddin Ayni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sadriddin Ayni (1878–1954) was a Tajik writer, poet and scholar, regarded as the founder of modern Tajik literature. Educated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, he joined the Jadid reform movement and later wrote novels and memoirs of pre-revolutionary Bukhara. He defended the place of Persian-Tajik culture in Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and in 1951 became the first president of the Tajik Academy of Sciences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni.jpg" alt="Sadriddin Ayni" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AyniSadriddin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sadriddin Ayni was born on 15 April 1878 into a peasant family in the village of Sāktare in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, now in Gʻijduvon District. He became an orphan at twelve and moved to Bukhara to join his older brother. There he attended the Mir-i Arab madrasa and learned to write in Arabic, working as a janitor and cook while he studied. He knew prominent Bukhara intellectuals and took part in the Jadid movement of enlighteners. From September 1915 to April 1916 he worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>-ginning plant at Kızıltepe station, where he met the workers who became the heroes of his first novels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In April 1917 reactionary forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a>, mostly clergy, staged a demonstration of loyalty in front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a>'s residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Ayni refused to take part. Seized at home, he was publicly beaten with 75 blows of sticks and thrown into the Obhona prison, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> rescued him. In the early 1920s he helped to spread the Russian Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, and in 1934 he attended the first Soviet Congress of Writers as the Tajik representative. He sat in the Supreme Soviet of Tajikistan for 20 years, received the Order of Lenin three times, and was the first president of the Academy of Sciences of the Tajik SSR.</p><p>By stressing national identity in his writing, he escaped the Soviet censors who silenced many intellectuals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. His early poems were about love and nature, but after the national awakening in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> he wrote of the dawn of the new age and of the working class.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His writings often criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Two well-known works are The Slave and The Bukhara Executioners. In 1927 he wrote Dokhunda, the first Tajikistani novel in the Tajik language. Lev Kuleshov worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> in 1934 and 1935 on a film of it, but the authorities suspected it might excite Tajik nationalism and stopped the project. A film of the book was approved in 1956. His four-volume Memoirs, completed in 1949–54, are widely read.</p><p>Ayni died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> on 15 July 1954 and is buried in a mausoleum in his honour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> regard him as the national poet, and after 1992 his writing helped to bind together a sense of Tajik nationalism that survived the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sadriddin Ayni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pupils memorised religious texts rather than having them explained: the Tajik Jadid <b>Sadriddin Ayni</b>, who attended a maktab in the 1890s, recalled that he learned the Arabic alphabet as an aid to memorisation but could not read a text he had not already memorised.</blockquote><a href="/movement/jadidism">Jadidism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Tajik literature flourished under writers such as <b>Sadriddin Ayni</b>, while the Pamirs formed the Gorno-Badakhshan Autonomous Oblast.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-ssr">Tajik SSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Emirate of Bukhara. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.57,68.79&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Dushanbe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6258 779 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sadriddin Ayni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6708 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6708 1049h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.57,68.79&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
